Basics about API which can be referred :-


API
• API = Medium through which Client and Server communicate.

REST
• REST = Standard for designing APIs.
• Resource-based URLs (/users, /products).
• Stateless → Every request is independent.
• Uses HTTP methods.

HTTP Methods
• GET → Read data.
• POST → Create data.
• PUT → Update entire resource.
• PATCH → Update specific fields.
• DELETE → Delete resource.

CRUD
• Create → POST
• Read → GET
• Update → PUT/PATCH
• Delete → DELETE

HTTP Status Codes
• 200 → OK
• 201 → Created
• 204 → No Content
• 400 → Bad Request
• 401 → Unauthorized (Not Authenticated)
• 403 → Forbidden (Authenticated but No Permission)
• 404 → Not Found
• 500 → Internal Server Error

Authentication vs Authorization
• Authentication → Who are you?
• Authorization → What are you allowed to do?

JWT (JSON Web Token)
• Stateless authentication.
• Client stores JWT; sends it with every request.
• Server verifies token.
• Structure → Header + Payload + Signature.
• Best for APIs, Mobile Apps, Microservices.

Session
• Stateful authentication.
• Server stores session; client stores Session ID (Cookie).
• Server checks session on every request.
• Best for Traditional Web Apps.

JWT vs Session
• JWT → Stateless, Scalable, No server storage.
• Session → Stateful, Easy logout, Requires server storage.


